<!--
    kuna issue `kuna-indirectonly-escape` (P6, sub-stage merge-aggressiveness).

    The counterexample that keeps the `indirectonly` option OFF by default.

    `kuna-indirectonly.xml` shows the sound direction: the machine stores a
    computed value INTO an escaped frame slot, and the merge only moves where
    that value is computed.  This is the other direction.  `walk` builds an
    object on its own frame, hands its address to three calls that fill it, then
    LOADS the element count out of it into r14d and counts r14d down; the slot
    itself is never written again, and after the loop the object's address goes
    to a fourth call:

      walk: sub rsp,0x20 / mov rbp,rsp          ; the object is at [rsp+0]
            call a_init(rbp,3) / call a_push(rbp,..) x3
            mov r14d,[rsp+8]                    ; count = obj.n, into a REGISTER
            sub r14d,1 / js 2f
        1:  mov rax,[rsp] / mov rdi,[rax+r14*8] / call sink / sub r14d,1
            cmp r14d,-1 / jne 1b
        2:  mov rdi,rbp / call a_done           ; a_done READS obj.n

    [rsp+8] is an illegal input (nothing in `walk` writes it before the load)
    and its entry value reaches nothing but the INDIRECTs of the three filling
    calls, so `check_indirect_use` accepts it.  With the flag set,
    `merge_test_adjacent` stops refusing and ActionMergeAdjacent folds r14d
    into the slot: the emitted C then decrements MEMORY the machine never writes
    and hands it to a_done.  Compiled against a real a_done, the two bodies
    disagree - the register form prints the count the binary passes, the merged
    form prints -1.

    The heritage pass cannot catch this.  A CPUI_INDIRECT is attached only where
    the storage is still live in the SSA, and this slot's last read is the load
    at the top, so there is no INDIRECT for it at `call sink` or at
    `call a_done` and the cover-intersection test has nothing to intersect.
    Stock Ghidra emits the same fabricated store on the same input, so kuna
    ships the port with the option off.

    Pass 1 runs the DEFAULT (no option command), so flipping the shipped default
    to on turns this test red.  Pass 2 asks for the option explicitly and pins
    what it costs.
-->
